192.168.197.2>ping

NAME
     ping - send ICMPv4 ECHO_REQUEST packets to network hosts

SYNOPSIS
     ping [-silent] [-ADbnrx] [-I ifname] [-S src_addr] [-T tsonly|tsandaddr|tsprespec]
          [-Q tos] [-V vr] [-c count] [-s packetsize] [-t ttl] [-w ms] host

DESCRIPTION
     ping uses the ICMPv4 protocol's mandatory ECHO_REQUEST datagram to
     elicit an ECHO_REPLY from a host or gateway.

     -A      Add router alert option to each sent ping request.
     -b      Allow pinging a broadcast address.
     -c count
             Stop after sending count ECHO_RESPONSE packets.
     -D      Set the DF (don't fragment) flag on each sent ping request.
     -F      Do not allow local fragmentation of packets.
     -I ifname
             Specify outgoing interface.
     -n      Numeric output only. No attempts will be made to look up
             names for host addresses.
     -Q tos  Specify the Type of Service field in the IPv4 header.
     -r      Bypass the normal routing tables and send directly to a host on
             an attached network.
     -R      Record route. Includes the RECORD_ROUTE option in the ECHO_REQUEST
             packet. IPNET will discard ECHO_REQUEST that contains RECORD_ROUTE
             Note2: the record route IP option cannot hold more than 9 hops.
             Many nodes ignore or discard this option.
     -s packetsize
             Specifies the number of data bytes to be sent. The default is
             56, which translates into 64 ICMP data bytes when combined with
             the 8 bytes of ICMP header data.
     -S src_addr  Specifies the address to use a source address when sending.
     -N addr Specifies the IP-address of the first hop.
     -t ttl  Use the specified time-to-live.
     -T timestamp options
             Include IP timestamp option. timestamp can be tsonly (only timestamp)
             tsandaddr (address and timestamp) or
             tsprespec host1 [host2 [host3 [host4]]] (timestamp specific hops)
     -x      Send a ICMP timestamp request instead of a ECHO request.
     -V vr   Specify virtual router. 0 if not specified.
     -w timeout in milliseconds, default is 1000 ms.
     -silent no output, returns success or failure.
             MUST be first argument.

192.168.197.2_XunXiqX

192.168.197.2>ping6

NAME
     ping6 - send ICMPv6 ECHO_REQUEST packets to network hosts

SYNOPSIS
     ping6 [-silent] [-Tnrt] [-c count] [-h hoplimit] [-m ifname] [-s packetsize] [-w timeout]
           [-F label] [-S src_addr] [-Q tclass] [-V vr] [hop ...] host

DESCRIPTION
     ping6 uses the ICMPv6 protocol's mandatory ICMP6_ECHO_REQUEST datagram to
     elicit an ICMP6_ECHO_REPLY from a host or gateway.  ICMP6_ECHO_REQUEST
     datagrams (``pings'') have an IPv6 header, and ICMPv6 header formatted as
     documented in RFC2463. It is possible to specify additional nodes that the
     ping request must visit before reaching the final destination.
     This is accomplished by adding a routing header to the packet.

     -c count
             Stop after sending count ECHO_RESPONSE packets.
     -F label
             Specifcy the flow label in the IPv6 header.
     -h hoplimit
             Set the IPv6 hoplimit.
     -I ifname
             Forces the packet to be sent on the specified interface.
     -m <ifname>
             Name of outgoing interface for multicast addresses.
     -N addr Uses 'addr' as the first-hop node.
     -n      Numeric output only. No attempts will be made to look up
             names for host addresses.
     -Q tclass
             Specify the traffic class in the IPv6 header.
     -r      Bypass the normal routing tables and send directly to a host on
             an attached network.
